const QUESTIONS=[
/* ===================== LEVEL 0 : EASY / BASIC ===================== */
{"id":"b1","level":0,"q":"What is Machine Learning?","a":"Machine Learning is a branch of AI that allows computers to learn patterns from data and make predictions or decisions without being explicitly programmed for every case. Example: predicting house prices from historical data."},
{"id":"b2","level":0,"q":"What is the difference between AI, ML, and Deep Learning?","a":"AI is a broad field of making machines perform intelligent tasks. Machine Learning is a subset of AI where machines learn from data. Deep Learning is a subset of ML that mainly uses multi-layer neural networks. AI > ML > DL."},
{"id":"b3","level":0,"q":"What are the main types of Machine Learning?","a":"Three main types: 1) Supervised Learning, 2) Unsupervised Learning, 3) Reinforcement Learning."},
{"id":"b4","level":0,"q":"What is Supervised Learning?","a":"A type of ML where the training data contains both input features and the correct target/output. Example: Hours Studied (2, 4, 6) -> Marks (40, 60, 80). Here marks are the target."},
{"id":"b5","level":0,"q":"What is Unsupervised Learning?","a":"Used when the dataset has no labeled target values. The model tries to discover patterns or groups in the data. Example: customer segmentation using K-Means clustering."},
{"id":"b6","level":0,"q":"What is Reinforcement Learning?","a":"A type of learning where an agent interacts with an environment and learns through rewards and penalties. Examples: game-playing AI, robot navigation, autonomous systems."},
{"id":"b7","level":0,"q":"What is Regression?","a":"Regression is used to predict a continuous numerical value. Examples: house price, salary, temperature, sales."},
{"id":"b8","level":0,"q":"What is Classification?","a":"Classification assigns data into predefined categories or classes. Examples: Email -> Spam / Not Spam; Tumor -> Benign / Malignant."},
{"id":"b9","level":0,"q":"What is Linear Regression?","a":"A supervised learning algorithm that predicts a continuous value by modeling a linear relationship between input features and the target. One feature: y = mx + c. Multiple features: y = b0 + b1x1 + b2x2 + ... + bnxn."},
{"id":"b10","level":0,"q":"What is Logistic Regression?","a":"Mainly used for classification. It predicts the probability of a class, commonly using the sigmoid function: sigma(z) = 1 / (1 + e^(-z))."},
{"id":"b11","level":0,"q":"What is the difference between Linear Regression and Logistic Regression?","a":"Linear Regression is used for regression, predicts continuous values, gives linear output and commonly uses MSE. Logistic Regression is mainly used for classification, predicts class probabilities, uses sigmoid for binary classification and commonly uses log loss."},
{"id":"b12","level":0,"q":"What is a Decision Tree?","a":"A supervised learning algorithm that makes predictions by recursively splitting data based on conditions. Example: Income > 50K? Yes -> Loan Yes, No -> Loan No."},
{"id":"b13","level":0,"q":"What is Gini Impurity?","a":"Gini Impurity measures how mixed the classes are within a node. Gini = 1 - sum(p_i^2). Lower Gini impurity means a purer node."},
{"id":"b14","level":0,"q":"What is Entropy?","a":"Entropy measures the uncertainty or impurity in a dataset. Entropy = -sum(p_i * log2(p_i))."},
{"id":"b15","level":0,"q":"What is Information Gain?","a":"Information Gain measures how much uncertainty is reduced after splitting the data. IG = Entropy(parent) - Weighted Entropy(children)."},
{"id":"b16","level":0,"q":"Why can a Decision Tree overfit?","a":"A very deep tree can learn noise and small details from training data instead of general patterns. Control it using max_depth, min_samples_split, min_samples_leaf and pruning."},
{"id":"b17","level":0,"q":"What is Random Forest?","a":"An ensemble learning algorithm that combines multiple Decision Trees to make a more robust prediction. For classification, trees generally use voting to determine the final class."},
{"id":"b18","level":0,"q":"What is Bagging?","a":"Bagging (Bootstrap Aggregating) trains multiple models on different bootstrap samples and combines their predictions. Random Forest is a popular example."},
{"id":"b19","level":0,"q":"What is Boosting?","a":"An ensemble technique where models are trained sequentially, with later models focusing on errors made by earlier models. Examples: AdaBoost, Gradient Boosting, XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost."},
{"id":"b20","level":0,"q":"What is the difference between Bagging and Boosting?","a":"Bagging: models are generally trained independently and their predictions combined. Boosting: models are trained sequentially, each one correcting the errors of the previous ones."},
{"id":"b21","level":0,"q":"What is Overfitting?","a":"Overfitting occurs when a model performs very well on training data but poorly on unseen data. Example: training accuracy 99%, testing accuracy 70%."},
{"id":"b22","level":0,"q":"What is Underfitting?","a":"Underfitting occurs when a model is too simple to learn the underlying patterns in the training data. Example: training accuracy 65%, testing accuracy 63%."},
{"id":"b23","level":0,"q":"How can you reduce overfitting?","a":"More representative training data, cross-validation, regularization, feature selection, data augmentation where appropriate, reducing model complexity, early stopping, and dropout for neural networks."},
{"id":"b24","level":0,"q":"What is Bias?","a":"Bias is the error caused by overly simplistic assumptions made by a model. High bias is generally associated with underfitting."},
{"id":"b25","level":0,"q":"What is Variance?","a":"Variance measures how sensitive a model is to changes in the training data. High variance is generally associated with overfitting."},
{"id":"b26","level":0,"q":"What is the Bias-Variance Tradeoff?","a":"The balance between underfitting and overfitting. High bias -> underfitting; high variance -> overfitting. The goal is a model that generalizes well to unseen data."},
{"id":"b27","level":0,"q":"What is Feature Scaling?","a":"Feature scaling transforms numerical features to comparable scales. Example: Age = 20-60 and Salary = 20,000-2,00,000. Without scaling, some algorithms give excessive importance to the larger-scale feature."},
{"id":"b28","level":0,"q":"What is Standardization?","a":"Standardization transforms data so it has mean 0 and standard deviation 1: z = (x - mean) / std. In Scikit-learn: StandardScaler."},
{"id":"b29","level":0,"q":"What is Normalization?","a":"Normalization scales values to a fixed range, often 0 to 1: x' = (x - x_min) / (x_max - x_min)."},
{"id":"b30","level":0,"q":"Which algorithms usually require feature scaling?","a":"Distance-based or gradient-based algorithms such as KNN, K-Means, SVM, Logistic Regression, Linear Regression and Neural Networks. Tree-based models (Decision Trees, Random Forests) generally do not need scaling."},
{"id":"b31","level":0,"q":"What are missing values?","a":"Values that are not available for some observations. Examples: NaN, None, NULL."},
{"id":"b32","level":0,"q":"How do you handle missing values?","a":"Numerical features: mean, median, or model-based imputation. Categorical features: mode or 'Unknown'. In some cases rows or columns may be removed if appropriate."},
{"id":"b33","level":0,"q":"What is an outlier?","a":"An observation significantly different from the normal pattern of the dataset. Example: salaries 30K, 32K, 35K, 31K and 5 Crore - the 5 Crore value could be an outlier depending on context."},
{"id":"b34","level":0,"q":"How do you detect outliers?","a":"Common methods: IQR method, Z-score, box plots, and domain knowledge."},
{"id":"b35","level":0,"q":"What is Train-Test Split?","a":"Dividing a dataset into training and testing subsets, e.g. 80% training and 20% testing. The training set trains the model; the test set is used for final evaluation on unseen data."},
{"id":"b36","level":0,"q":"What is a Validation Set?","a":"A set used during model development to tune hyperparameters and compare models. Training -> Learn, Validation -> Tune, Testing -> Final evaluation."},
{"id":"b37","level":0,"q":"What is Data Leakage?","a":"Data leakage occurs when information that should not be available during training is accidentally used by the model, resulting in unrealistically high evaluation performance."},
{"id":"b38","level":0,"q":"Give an example of Data Leakage.","a":"Predicting whether a patient will be hospitalized but accidentally using information recorded after the hospitalization. The model is using future information, which is data leakage."},
{"id":"b39","level":0,"q":"What is Cross-Validation?","a":"A technique for evaluating a model using multiple train-validation splits. The most common approach is K-Fold Cross-Validation."},
{"id":"b40","level":0,"q":"What is K-Fold Cross-Validation?","a":"The dataset is divided into K folds. The model is trained on K-1 folds and validated on the remaining fold. This repeats until every fold has been used for validation."},
{"id":"b41","level":0,"q":"Why do we use Cross-Validation?","a":"It gives a more reliable estimate of model performance and reduces dependence on a single train-test split."},
{"id":"b42","level":0,"q":"What is Accuracy?","a":"Accuracy = Correct Predictions / Total Predictions. If a model makes 90 correct predictions out of 100, accuracy = 90%."},
{"id":"b43","level":0,"q":"What is Precision?","a":"Precision = TP / (TP + FP). Of all samples predicted as positive, how many were actually positive?"},
{"id":"b44","level":0,"q":"What is Recall?","a":"Recall = TP / (TP + FN). Of all actual positive samples, how many did the model correctly identify?"},
{"id":"b45","level":0,"q":"What is F1 Score?","a":"The harmonic mean of Precision and Recall: F1 = 2 * (Precision * Recall) / (Precision + Recall). Useful when both precision and recall are important."},
{"id":"b46","level":0,"q":"What is a Confusion Matrix?","a":"A table summarizing classification predictions: Actual Positive/Predicted Positive = TP, Actual Positive/Predicted Negative = FN, Actual Negative/Predicted Positive = FP, Actual Negative/Predicted Negative = TN."},
{"id":"b47","level":0,"q":"What is a False Positive?","a":"The actual class is negative but the model predicts positive. Example: a normal email marked as spam."},
{"id":"b48","level":0,"q":"What is a False Negative?","a":"The actual class is positive but the model predicts negative. Example: a spam email marked as normal."},
{"id":"b49","level":0,"q":"What is Gradient Descent?","a":"An optimization algorithm that minimizes a loss function by updating model parameters in the direction that reduces the loss: compute loss -> compute gradient -> update parameters -> lower loss."},
{"id":"b50","level":0,"q":"What is Learning Rate?","a":"Learning rate determines how large each parameter update is during optimization. Too high: training may become unstable. Too low: training becomes very slow."},
{"id":"b51","level":0,"q":"What is an Epoch?","a":"One complete pass through the entire training dataset. With 10,000 training samples, 1 epoch = the model sees all 10,000 samples once."},
{"id":"b52","level":0,"q":"What is Batch Size?","a":"The number of training samples processed together before a model update. Example: dataset = 10,000 samples, batch size = 32, so data is processed in batches of 32."},
{"id":"b53","level":0,"q":"What is the difference between Parameters and Hyperparameters?","a":"Parameters are learned by the model during training (weights, biases). Hyperparameters are set before or around training and control the learning process (learning rate, number of trees, max depth, K in KNN)."},
{"id":"b54","level":0,"q":"What is Hyperparameter Tuning?","a":"Finding hyperparameter values that improve model performance on validation data. Common techniques: Grid Search, Random Search, Bayesian Optimization."},
{"id":"b55","level":0,"q":"What is KNN?","a":"K-Nearest Neighbors makes predictions based on the closest data points. For classification, it commonly uses majority voting among the nearest neighbors."},
{"id":"b56","level":0,"q":"What does K represent in KNN?","a":"K is the number of nearest neighbors considered for making a prediction. K = 5 means the model considers the 5 nearest points."},
{"id":"b57","level":0,"q":"What is K-Means?","a":"An unsupervised clustering algorithm that divides data into K clusters based on similarity."},
{"id":"b58","level":0,"q":"What is the Elbow Method?","a":"A method to select a suitable number of clusters by examining how clustering cost (commonly inertia) changes as K increases; the 'elbow' point is a good choice."},
{"id":"b59","level":0,"q":"What is PCA?","a":"Principal Component Analysis is a dimensionality reduction technique that transforms high-dimensional data into fewer components while preserving as much variance as possible."},
{"id":"b60","level":0,"q":"Why is PCA used?","a":"To reduce dimensionality, improve visualization, reduce computational cost, remove some redundancy, and create compact representations."},
{"id":"b61","level":0,"q":"What is Regularization?","a":"A technique to reduce overfitting by adding a penalty for model complexity. Common types: L1 and L2 regularization."},
{"id":"b62","level":0,"q":"What is the difference between L1 and L2 Regularization?","a":"L1 adds lambda * sum(|w|) to the loss and can make some weights exactly zero (feature selection). L2 adds lambda * sum(w^2) and generally shrinks weights toward zero."},
{"id":"b63","level":0,"q":"Your training accuracy is 99% but testing accuracy is 70%. What would you do?","a":"This could indicate overfitting. First check for data leakage and validation performance. Then consider reducing model complexity, applying regularization, using cross-validation, improving training data, or performing feature selection."},
{"id":"b64","level":0,"q":"Both training and testing accuracy are low. What could be the reason?","a":"This could indicate underfitting. Check feature quality, preprocessing, model capacity and hyperparameters."},
{"id":"b65","level":0,"q":"Your dataset is 95% normal and 5% fraud, and accuracy is 95%. Is the model good?","a":"Not necessarily. A model predicting every sample as normal gets 95% accuracy. For imbalanced problems also examine precision, recall, F1-score, the confusion matrix and potentially PR-AUC."},
{"id":"b66","level":0,"q":"How can you handle an imbalanced dataset?","a":"Class weights, oversampling, undersampling, SMOTE, threshold tuning, and appropriate evaluation metrics. The choice depends on the problem and the cost of different types of errors."},
{"id":"b67","level":0,"q":"Tell me about your ML project.","a":"Answer structure: 'My project solves [problem]. I used [dataset] and performed data preprocessing and feature engineering. I trained [model] and evaluated it using [metrics]. Finally, I integrated the trained model into [application/API/deployment platform].'"},
{"id":"b68","level":0,"q":"Why did you choose this model?","a":"I selected it based on the characteristics of the problem, dataset size, interpretability, computational requirements and validation performance. I also considered alternative models where appropriate."},
{"id":"b69","level":0,"q":"How did you preprocess your data?","a":"I inspected the dataset for missing values, duplicates, incorrect data types and potential outliers. Then I performed the necessary encoding, scaling and feature engineering based on the model's requirements. Only mention steps you actually performed."},
{"id":"b70","level":0,"q":"Why did you choose this evaluation metric?","a":"I selected the metric based on the business objective and the type of errors that matter. For example, in fraud detection accuracy alone may not be enough because the dataset can be highly imbalanced."},
{"id":"b71","level":0,"q":"How would you deploy your ML model?","a":"Save the trained model using a serialization method, create a prediction API with a framework such as Flask or FastAPI, connect it to a frontend if required, and deploy on a suitable cloud platform."},

/* ===================== LEVEL 1 : MEDIUM ===================== */
{"id":"m1","level":1,"q":"What is the difference between a model and an algorithm?","a":"An algorithm is the method used to learn from data; a model is the learned representation produced after training. Linear Regression is an algorithm; after training, the learned coefficients form the model."},
{"id":"m2","level":1,"q":"What is generalization in Machine Learning?","a":"The ability of a trained model to perform well on unseen data, not just training data. A good model learns general patterns rather than memorizing the training set."},
{"id":"m3","level":1,"q":"What is the difference between training error and test error?","a":"Training error is the error on the data used to train the model; test error is the error on unseen data. A large gap between them can indicate overfitting."},
{"id":"m4","level":1,"q":"What is empirical risk?","a":"The average loss of a model on the training dataset. It measures how badly the model performs on the observed training examples."},
{"id":"m5","level":1,"q":"What is a baseline model?","a":"A simple model used as a reference point before trying complex models. Example: predicting the majority class for a classification problem."},
{"id":"m6","level":1,"q":"Why should we establish a baseline before training complex models?","a":"It gives a reference performance. If a complex model does not significantly improve over the baseline, its extra complexity may not be justified."},
{"id":"m7","level":1,"q":"What is model capacity?","a":"The ability of a model to learn complex patterns. Very low capacity may underfit; very high capacity may overfit."},
{"id":"m8","level":1,"q":"What is the difference between parametric and non-parametric models?","a":"Parametric models assume a fixed form and have a fixed number of parameters (Linear Regression, Logistic Regression). Non-parametric models can grow more complex as more data becomes available (KNN, Decision Trees)."},
{"id":"m9","level":1,"q":"What is a deterministic model?","a":"A model that produces the same output for the same input and model state."},
{"id":"m10","level":1,"q":"What is stochasticity in Machine Learning?","a":"Randomness involved in some part of the learning process. Examples: random initialization, random train-test splitting, Random Forest sampling, Stochastic Gradient Descent."},
{"id":"m11","level":1,"q":"Why should we not train and test a model on the same data?","a":"The model may memorize the training data, giving an overly optimistic performance estimate. We need unseen data to measure generalization."},
{"id":"m12","level":1,"q":"What is stratified sampling?","a":"Ensuring important groups or classes keep approximately the same proportion in different subsets. Stratified train-test splitting preserves the class distribution."},
{"id":"m13","level":1,"q":"When should you use K-Fold Cross-Validation?","a":"When you want a more reliable estimate of model performance, especially when the dataset is not very large."},
{"id":"m14","level":1,"q":"What is Stratified K-Fold Cross-Validation?","a":"K-Fold Cross-Validation where each fold approximately preserves the class distribution of the original dataset. Particularly useful for classification."},
{"id":"m15","level":1,"q":"What is Leave-One-Out Cross-Validation?","a":"One sample is used for validation while all remaining samples are used for training, repeated for every sample. It can be computationally expensive for large datasets."},
{"id":"m16","level":1,"q":"Can we use the test set for hyperparameter tuning?","a":"No. The test set should be kept untouched until final evaluation. Tune hyperparameters using training data with validation or cross-validation."},
{"id":"m17","level":1,"q":"What is nested cross-validation?","a":"Two levels of cross-validation: the inner loop tunes hyperparameters and the outer loop gives an unbiased model evaluation. Useful to estimate generalization while tuning."},
{"id":"m18","level":1,"q":"What is target leakage?","a":"When a feature contains information that directly or indirectly reveals the target and would not be available at prediction time."},
{"id":"m19","level":1,"q":"How can preprocessing cause data leakage?","a":"If scaling or imputation is fitted on the entire dataset before splitting, test-set information influences the transformation. Preprocessing should be fitted only on training data."},
{"id":"m20","level":1,"q":"How can you prevent data leakage?","a":"Split data before preprocessing, fit transformations only on training data, use pipelines, avoid future information, and keep the final test set untouched."},
{"id":"m21","level":1,"q":"What is Feature Engineering?","a":"Creating, transforming or selecting features to help a model learn useful patterns from the data."},
{"id":"m22","level":1,"q":"Why is feature engineering important?","a":"Good features make patterns easier for a model to learn and can improve performance significantly, sometimes more than changing the algorithm."},
{"id":"m23","level":1,"q":"What is feature selection?","a":"Selecting the most useful features and removing irrelevant or redundant ones. Benefits: reduced computation, lower overfitting risk, better interpretability, sometimes better generalization."},
{"id":"m24","level":1,"q":"What is the difference between feature selection and feature extraction?","a":"Feature selection picks existing features; feature extraction creates new features from existing ones. PCA is a feature extraction technique."},
{"id":"m25","level":1,"q":"What is multicollinearity?","a":"When two or more input features are highly correlated. It can make coefficient estimates unstable in models like Linear Regression."},
{"id":"m26","level":1,"q":"How can you detect multicollinearity?","a":"Correlation matrix, Variance Inflation Factor (VIF), and domain knowledge."},
{"id":"m27","level":1,"q":"How can you handle multicollinearity?","a":"Remove highly correlated features, combine features, use dimensionality reduction, or apply regularization such as L2."},
{"id":"m28","level":1,"q":"Why can mean imputation be problematic?","a":"It reduces the natural variance of the feature and is strongly affected by outliers. Median imputation may be more robust for skewed data."},
{"id":"m29","level":1,"q":"When should you not remove an outlier?","a":"Not automatically. It could be a genuine rare event, important business information or a valid extreme case. First understand why it exists."},
{"id":"m30","level":1,"q":"What is the IQR method for detecting outliers?","a":"IQR = Q3 - Q1. Observations below Q1 - 1.5*IQR or above Q3 + 1.5*IQR are potential outliers."},
{"id":"m31","level":1,"q":"Why does feature scaling matter for KNN?","a":"KNN uses distance calculations, so a feature with a much larger numerical scale can dominate the distance."},
{"id":"m32","level":1,"q":"Why doesn't a Decision Tree usually require scaling?","a":"Trees split data using feature thresholds rather than distances or magnitudes, so feature scale does not matter in the same way."},
{"id":"m33","level":1,"q":"What is Robust Scaling?","a":"Scaling using the median and IQR, which makes it less sensitive to outliers than standard scaling."},
{"id":"m34","level":1,"q":"When would you use RobustScaler instead of StandardScaler?","a":"When numerical features contain significant outliers and I want scaling to be less affected by them."},
{"id":"m35","level":1,"q":"What is log transformation?","a":"Transforming a variable using a logarithmic function. It can reduce strong right-skewness and compress large values."},
{"id":"m36","level":1,"q":"What assumptions does Linear Regression make?","a":"Linear relationship, independent observations, constant error variance, low or manageable multicollinearity, and normally distributed residuals for certain statistical inference."},
{"id":"m37","level":1,"q":"What are residuals?","a":"The difference between the actual and predicted value: Residual = y - y_hat."},
{"id":"m38","level":1,"q":"What is R-squared?","a":"The proportion of variance in the target explained by the regression model. R2 = 1 - SS_res / SS_tot."},
{"id":"m39","level":1,"q":"What is Adjusted R-squared?","a":"R-squared adjusted for the number of predictors. It can decrease when unnecessary features are added."},
{"id":"m40","level":1,"q":"Why can R-squared be misleading?","a":"It can increase when extra features are added even if they don't meaningfully improve the model, and by itself it doesn't guarantee good predictions on unseen data."},
{"id":"m41","level":1,"q":"What is Mean Absolute Error?","a":"MAE = (1/n) * sum(|y - y_hat|). The average absolute prediction error."},
{"id":"m42","level":1,"q":"What is Mean Squared Error?","a":"MSE = (1/n) * sum((y - y_hat)^2). Because errors are squared, large errors get more weight."},
{"id":"m43","level":1,"q":"What is RMSE?","a":"RMSE = sqrt(MSE). It has the same units as the target and penalizes larger errors more strongly than MAE."},
{"id":"m44","level":1,"q":"When would you prefer MAE over RMSE?","a":"When you want a metric that is less sensitive to large errors and easier to interpret directly in the target's units."},
{"id":"m45","level":1,"q":"What is the difference between L1 and L2 regularization in Linear Regression?","a":"L1 can make some coefficients exactly zero (useful for sparse solutions). L2 shrinks coefficients toward zero but usually not exactly to zero."},
{"id":"m46","level":1,"q":"Why is accuracy not always a good metric?","a":"It can be misleading with imbalanced data. If 99% of transactions are legitimate, predicting all as legitimate gives 99% accuracy but detects no fraud."},
{"id":"m47","level":1,"q":"When is precision more important than recall?","a":"When false positives are particularly costly, e.g. if incorrectly flagging legitimate emails as spam has a high cost."},
{"id":"m48","level":1,"q":"When is recall more important than precision?","a":"When missing a positive case is particularly costly, e.g. medical screening where missing a true positive is worse than extra false positives."},
{"id":"m49","level":1,"q":"What is a classification threshold?","a":"A cutoff that converts a predicted probability into a class label. With threshold 0.5: probability >= 0.5 -> Positive, otherwise Negative. The right threshold depends on the application."},
{"id":"m50","level":1,"q":"What happens if you lower the classification threshold?","a":"More samples are classified as positive. Recall usually increases, but false positives may increase and precision may drop."},
{"id":"m51","level":1,"q":"What is ROC-AUC?","a":"It measures how well a classifier separates positive and negative classes across all thresholds. Higher AUC generally means better ranking/discrimination ability."},
{"id":"m52","level":1,"q":"What is PR-AUC?","a":"The area under the Precision-Recall curve. It is particularly informative for highly imbalanced classification problems."},
{"id":"m53","level":1,"q":"What is the difference between ROC-AUC and PR-AUC?","a":"ROC-AUC uses True Positive Rate and False Positive Rate; PR-AUC uses Precision and Recall. For heavily imbalanced data, PR-AUC gives a more informative view of positive-class performance."},
{"id":"m54","level":1,"q":"How does a Decision Tree select a split?","a":"It evaluates candidate splits using Gini impurity, Entropy/Information Gain, or variance reduction (regression) and picks the split that best improves the objective."},
{"id":"m55","level":1,"q":"What is pruning in Decision Trees?","a":"Removing unnecessary branches to reduce complexity and help prevent overfitting."},
{"id":"m56","level":1,"q":"What happens if the maximum depth of a Decision Tree is too large?","a":"The tree becomes very complex and may memorize training data, leading to overfitting."},
{"id":"m57","level":1,"q":"What happens if the maximum depth is too small?","a":"The tree becomes too simple, misses important patterns and underfits."},
{"id":"m58","level":1,"q":"Why does Random Forest reduce overfitting compared with a single Decision Tree?","a":"It combines many diverse trees trained with randomness in samples and features. Averaging their predictions reduces variance compared with a single tree."},
{"id":"m59","level":1,"q":"What is feature randomness in Random Forest?","a":"At each split, Random Forest considers only a random subset of features instead of all features, which creates diverse trees."},
{"id":"m60","level":1,"q":"What is out-of-bag evaluation?","a":"In Random Forest, samples not selected in a particular bootstrap sample are called out-of-bag samples and can be used to estimate model performance."},
{"id":"m61","level":1,"q":"What is Gradient Boosting?","a":"Builds models sequentially, where each new model tries to reduce the errors of the existing ensemble by optimizing the loss function."},
{"id":"m62","level":1,"q":"What is XGBoost?","a":"An optimized implementation of gradient-boosted decision trees with techniques for efficient training and regularization."},
{"id":"m63","level":1,"q":"Random Forest vs XGBoost?","a":"Random Forest: bagging-based, trees trained largely independently, often a strong easy baseline. XGBoost: boosting-based, trees built sequentially, often needs more careful tuning. The choice depends on dataset and problem."},
{"id":"m64","level":1,"q":"Why is KNN called a lazy learner?","a":"It does not build an explicit model during training; it stores the training data and does most of the computation at prediction time."},
{"id":"m65","level":1,"q":"What happens if K is too small in KNN?","a":"The model becomes highly sensitive to individual points and noise, increasing variance."},
{"id":"m66","level":1,"q":"What happens if K is too large in KNN?","a":"The model becomes too smooth and may ignore local patterns, increasing bias."},
{"id":"m67","level":1,"q":"What is the main idea behind K-Means?","a":"Divide data into K clusters by assigning points to the nearest centroid and repeatedly updating the centroids."},
{"id":"m68","level":1,"q":"What is a centroid in K-Means?","a":"The center point representing a cluster, typically the mean of the points assigned to it."},
{"id":"m69","level":1,"q":"What is a limitation of K-Means?","a":"K must be chosen in advance, and it performs poorly with irregular cluster shapes, very different cluster sizes, or strong outliers."},
{"id":"m70","level":1,"q":"What is SVM?","a":"Support Vector Machine is a supervised algorithm that finds a decision boundary separating classes while maximizing the margin between them."},
{"id":"m71","level":1,"q":"What are support vectors?","a":"Training points closest to the decision boundary. They strongly influence the position of the SVM boundary."},
{"id":"m72","level":1,"q":"What is the kernel trick?","a":"It lets SVM model nonlinear relationships by implicitly mapping data into a higher-dimensional space without explicitly computing the transformation. Common kernels: Linear, Polynomial, RBF."},
{"id":"m73","level":1,"q":"What is Batch Gradient Descent?","a":"Calculates the gradient using the entire training dataset before each parameter update."},
{"id":"m74","level":1,"q":"What is Stochastic Gradient Descent?","a":"Updates parameters using one training sample at a time. Faster for large datasets but has noisier updates."},
{"id":"m75","level":1,"q":"What is Mini-Batch Gradient Descent?","a":"Calculates updates using a small batch of samples. It combines advantages of batch and stochastic approaches and is widely used in ML and deep learning."},
{"id":"m76","level":1,"q":"What happens if the learning rate is too high?","a":"Optimization may overshoot the minimum, oscillate, or even diverge."},
{"id":"m77","level":1,"q":"What happens if the learning rate is too low?","a":"Training becomes extremely slow and may take many iterations to converge."},
{"id":"m78","level":1,"q":"What problem does PCA solve?","a":"It reduces the number of dimensions while preserving as much variance in the data as possible."},
{"id":"m79","level":1,"q":"What is a principal component?","a":"A new direction in feature space that captures a certain amount of variance. The first principal component captures the maximum variance."},
{"id":"m80","level":1,"q":"Why should data often be scaled before PCA?","a":"PCA is sensitive to feature scales; a feature with a much larger scale can dominate the variance calculation."},
{"id":"m81","level":1,"q":"What is Grid Search?","a":"Evaluates predefined combinations of hyperparameters and selects the one with the best validation or cross-validation performance."},
{"id":"m82","level":1,"q":"What is Random Search?","a":"Samples hyperparameter combinations from specified distributions instead of testing every combination. More efficient when only some hyperparameters strongly affect performance."},
{"id":"m83","level":1,"q":"Grid Search vs Random Search?","a":"Grid Search tests every specified combination and can become expensive. Random Search tests randomly selected combinations and explores large spaces more efficiently."},
{"id":"m84","level":1,"q":"What is Bayesian Optimization?","a":"Uses previous evaluation results to intelligently choose promising hyperparameter configurations instead of searching blindly."},
{"id":"m85","level":1,"q":"What is feature importance?","a":"An indication of how much individual features contribute to a model's predictions according to a particular method. Different models and methods calculate it differently."},
{"id":"m86","level":1,"q":"What is SHAP?","a":"SHapley Additive exPlanations - a method for explaining model predictions by assigning contribution values to features."},
{"id":"m87","level":1,"q":"Why is model interpretability important?","a":"It helps understand predictions, debug models, detect unexpected behavior, build trust, and meet domain or regulatory requirements."},
{"id":"m88","level":1,"q":"What is an ML Pipeline?","a":"A single workflow combining multiple preprocessing and modeling steps. Example: Raw Data -> Imputation -> Scaling -> Feature Selection -> Model."},
{"id":"m89","level":1,"q":"Why are pipelines useful?","a":"They prevent preprocessing inconsistencies, reduce data leakage, keep training and inference transformations consistent, and simplify experimentation."},
{"id":"m90","level":1,"q":"What is model serialization?","a":"Saving a trained model so it can be loaded later for prediction without retraining. Examples: joblib, pickle, framework-specific formats."},
{"id":"m91","level":1,"q":"What is inference?","a":"Using a trained model to generate predictions on new data."},
{"id":"m92","level":1,"q":"What is model drift?","a":"When the relationship between input data and the target changes over time, causing model performance to decrease."},
{"id":"m93","level":1,"q":"What is data drift?","a":"When the distribution of input features changes over time. Example: a spam classifier trained on 2025 emails faces very different vocabulary and spam patterns in 2027."},
{"id":"m94","level":1,"q":"What is concept drift?","a":"When the relationship between features and the target changes over time. Example: customer purchasing behavior changes significantly over several years."},
{"id":"m95","level":1,"q":"Your model performs well offline but poorly after deployment. What would you check?","a":"Training-serving data differences, preprocessing consistency, data leakage, feature order/names, missing-value handling, distribution shift, model version, and production input quality."},
{"id":"m96","level":1,"q":"Your model has high precision but low recall. What does it mean?","a":"The model is conservative about predicting positives: when it predicts positive it is usually correct, but it misses many actual positives."},
{"id":"m97","level":1,"q":"Your model has high recall but low precision. What does it mean?","a":"The model identifies most actual positives but also produces many false positives."},
{"id":"m98","level":1,"q":"Your model's accuracy is good but F1-score is poor. Why?","a":"Likely an imbalanced dataset. Accuracy is dominated by the majority class, while F1 reflects the balance between precision and recall."},
{"id":"m99","level":1,"q":"Your model performs differently on different random train-test splits. Why?","a":"The dataset may be small, noisy or contain sensitive samples, so performance varies with the samples in each split. Cross-validation gives a more stable estimate."},
{"id":"m100","level":1,"q":"You have only 500 labeled samples. What would you do?","a":"Understand the problem and data quality first. Then consider cross-validation, simpler models, strong feature engineering, regularization, data augmentation, collecting more labels, and transfer learning if suitable. Avoid blindly using a highly complex model."},

/* ===================== LEVEL 2 : HARD ===================== */
{"id":"h1","level":2,"q":"What is the difference between empirical risk minimization and structural risk minimization?","a":"ERM minimizes training error: min_f (1/n) sum L(y_i, f(x_i)). SRM also considers model complexity to reduce overfitting, i.e. training error + complexity control. Regularization is a practical way to implement SRM."},
{"id":"h2","level":2,"q":"Why can a model with zero training error still perform poorly?","a":"It may have overfit, learning noise, outliers, accidental patterns or irrelevant features instead of the true relationship. Training error near 0 does not guarantee test error near 0."},
{"id":"h3","level":2,"q":"Explain the bias-variance tradeoff mathematically.","a":"For squared-error regression: Expected error = Bias^2 + Variance + Irreducible Noise. Bias comes from overly simple assumptions, variance from sensitivity to training data, noise cannot be eliminated. Increasing complexity usually lowers bias and raises variance; aim for a good balance."},
{"id":"h4","level":2,"q":"Can increasing model complexity ever reduce both training and test error?","a":"Yes, if the original model is underfitting. Example: simple model train 30%/test 32% error, better model train 15%/test 18%. More complexity does not automatically mean overfitting."},
{"id":"h5","level":2,"q":"What is double descent?","a":"A phenomenon where test error decreases, then increases near the interpolation threshold, then decreases again as model complexity keeps growing. It challenges the simple U-shaped curve and is seen in highly overparameterized models."},
{"id":"h6","level":2,"q":"What is the interpolation threshold?","a":"Roughly the point where a model can fit the training data almost perfectly. Near it, behavior can be unstable and test error may rise; it is key to understanding double descent."},
{"id":"h7","level":2,"q":"What is the curse of dimensionality?","a":"As features increase, data becomes sparse in feature space: distances become less meaningful, more data is needed, computation grows and overfitting risk rises. KNN can perform poorly with many irrelevant dimensions."},
{"id":"h8","level":2,"q":"Why do distances become less meaningful in high dimensions?","a":"The gap between nearest and farthest points becomes relatively small: (d_max - d_min) / d_min shrinks as dimensionality grows. This is called distance concentration."},
{"id":"h9","level":2,"q":"Why does regularization help generalization?","a":"It restricts the model from learning excessively complex solutions. L2 minimizes Loss + lambda * sum(w^2), penalizing large weights, encouraging smoother models that often perform better on unseen data."},
{"id":"h10","level":2,"q":"Why does ordinary least squares have a closed-form solution?","a":"Minimize J(b) = ||y - Xb||^2. Derivative: -2X^T(y - Xb) = 0 gives X^T X b = X^T y, so b = (X^T X)^(-1) X^T y, when the inverse exists."},
{"id":"h11","level":2,"q":"What happens when X^T X is not invertible?","a":"It happens with perfect multicollinearity or redundant features. Use the pseudo-inverse, Ridge, QR decomposition or SVD. Ridge helps because b = (X^T X + lambda I)^(-1) X^T y makes the matrix better conditioned."},
{"id":"h12","level":2,"q":"Why is Ridge more stable than ordinary linear regression under multicollinearity?","a":"Ridge adds lambda*I to X^T X, making it better conditioned and reducing the sensitivity of coefficients to small changes in the data."},
{"id":"h13","level":2,"q":"Why can Lasso produce exactly zero coefficients?","a":"Lasso uses L1 penalty lambda * sum(|w|). The absolute value has a sharp point at zero, so optimization can drive some w_j exactly to 0, giving feature selection."},
{"id":"h14","level":2,"q":"Why does Ridge usually not produce exact zero coefficients?","a":"The L2 penalty lambda * sum(w^2) smoothly shrinks coefficients toward zero but generally does not make them exactly zero."},
{"id":"h15","level":2,"q":"What is Elastic Net?","a":"Combines L1 and L2 regularization: Loss + l1*sum|w| + l2*sum(w^2). It gives feature selection from L1 and stability with correlated features from L2."},
{"id":"h16","level":2,"q":"What is heteroscedasticity?","a":"When the variance of residuals changes with the predicted value or another variable (e.g. errors spread out as income increases). It violates a classical linear regression assumption."},
{"id":"h17","level":2,"q":"How would you detect heteroscedasticity?","a":"Residual vs fitted plot (funnel shape), Breusch-Pagan test, White test."},
{"id":"h18","level":2,"q":"How can heteroscedasticity be handled?","a":"Log-transform the target, transform features, use weighted least squares, robust standard errors, or a model less sensitive to this assumption."},
{"id":"h19","level":2,"q":"What is autocorrelation in regression residuals?","a":"Residuals are correlated with previous residuals (e_t correlated with e_(t-1)), common in time series. It violates the independence assumption."},
{"id":"h20","level":2,"q":"Why is logistic regression called regression if it performs classification?","a":"Because it models a continuous quantity - the probability P(y=1|x) - using the sigmoid function; the probability is then converted to a class using a threshold."},
{"id":"h21","level":2,"q":"What does a coefficient of logistic regression mean?","a":"The change in log-odds for a one-unit increase in the feature, holding others constant: log(p/(1-p)) = b0 + b1x. Exponentiating gives the odds ratio e^(b1)."},
{"id":"h22","level":2,"q":"Why is MSE generally not preferred for logistic regression?","a":"The standard objective is log loss/cross-entropy: L = -[y log p + (1-y) log(1-p)]. It comes from maximum likelihood for Bernoulli outcomes and gives a suitable optimization objective."},
{"id":"h23","level":2,"q":"How does logistic regression handle multiclass classification?","a":"One-vs-Rest, or multinomial logistic regression with softmax: P(y=k|x) = e^(z_k) / sum_j e^(z_j)."},
{"id":"h24","level":2,"q":"What is probability calibration?","a":"A classifier is calibrated if predicted probabilities match actual frequencies (among predictions of 0.8, about 80% are truly positive). A model can have good accuracy but poor calibration."},
{"id":"h25","level":2,"q":"Why are decision trees prone to overfitting?","a":"They can keep splitting until they memorize individual training examples. Controls: max_depth, min_samples_split, min_samples_leaf, max_leaf_nodes, pruning."},
{"id":"h26","level":2,"q":"What is cost-complexity pruning?","a":"Minimizes R(T) + alpha*|T|, where R(T) is tree error, |T| the number of terminal nodes and alpha the complexity penalty. Larger alpha gives a simpler tree."},
{"id":"h27","level":2,"q":"What is the difference between Gini impurity and entropy?","a":"Gini = 1 - sum p_i^2; Entropy = -sum p_i log p_i. Both measure node impurity and often produce similar trees in practice."},
{"id":"h28","level":2,"q":"Why don't decision trees require feature scaling?","a":"Trees decide using thresholds (x_j < t). Changing feature scale usually doesn't change the ordering of values, so normalization is generally unnecessary."},
{"id":"h29","level":2,"q":"Why can decision trees handle nonlinear relationships?","a":"They recursively divide the feature space into regions, e.g. if age < 30 and income > 50K then class A, creating nonlinear decision boundaries."},
{"id":"h30","level":2,"q":"Why does Random Forest reduce variance?","a":"Random Forest combines many diverse decision trees using bootstrap samples and random feature subsets. Averaging their predictions reduces the effect of individual tree errors, lowering variance."},
{"id":"h31","level":2,"q":"Why does Random Forest use random feature subsets?","a":"If every tree used all features, trees would be highly correlated. Random subsets create diverse trees, and lower correlation makes averaging more effective."},
{"id":"h32","level":2,"q":"What is the difference between bagging and boosting?","a":"Bagging: models trained independently, mainly reduces variance (Random Forest). Boosting: models trained sequentially, later models focus on previous errors, often reduces bias (AdaBoost, Gradient Boosting, XGBoost)."},
{"id":"h33","level":2,"q":"How does AdaBoost work?","a":"It gives more weight to misclassified examples so the next weak learner focuses on difficult ones. The final prediction is a weighted combination of weak learners."},
{"id":"h34","level":2,"q":"How is Gradient Boosting different from AdaBoost?","a":"AdaBoost changes example weights based on errors. Gradient Boosting fits each new model to the negative gradient of the loss and adds it to the ensemble."},
{"id":"h35","level":2,"q":"Why can boosting overfit?","a":"Too many trees, very deep trees, high learning rate or weak regularization can make the ensemble fit noise. Controls: learning rate, depth, number of estimators, subsampling, regularization, early stopping."},
{"id":"h36","level":2,"q":"What is early stopping in boosting?","a":"Training stops when validation performance stops improving (e.g. validation loss 0.31 -> 0.25 -> 0.24 -> 0.24 -> 0.25), so we stop near the best validation point."},
{"id":"h37","level":2,"q":"What is stacking?","a":"Combining predictions from multiple different models (e.g. Random Forest, XGBoost, Logistic Regression) and feeding them into a meta-model that learns how to combine them."},
{"id":"h38","level":2,"q":"What is blending?","a":"Combining predictions from multiple models, usually using a validation set to train or decide the combination. Similar to stacking but with a simpler setup."},
{"id":"h39","level":2,"q":"What is the margin in SVM?","a":"The distance between the decision boundary and the closest training points (support vectors). SVM tries to maximize it."},
{"id":"h40","level":2,"q":"What is a hard-margin SVM?","a":"Assumes data is perfectly linearly separable and allows no classification errors. Rarely suitable for noisy real-world data."},
{"id":"h41","level":2,"q":"What is a soft-margin SVM?","a":"Allows some margin violations. Parameter C controls the tradeoff: large C penalizes mistakes strongly (narrower margin); small C allows more violations (wider margin)."},
{"id":"h42","level":2,"q":"What does gamma mean in an RBF kernel?","a":"Gamma controls how far a training point's influence extends. High gamma: local influence, complex boundary, possible overfitting. Low gamma: broader influence, smoother boundary, possible underfitting."},
{"id":"h43","level":2,"q":"What is the kernel trick? (advanced)","a":"It lets SVM model nonlinear relationships without explicitly computing the high-dimensional transformation. Instead of computing phi(x)^T phi(z), we directly compute K(x, z). Examples: RBF, polynomial, sigmoid, linear."},
{"id":"h44","level":2,"q":"What assumption does Naive Bayes make?","a":"Features are conditionally independent given the class: P(x1..xn|y) = product of P(xi|y). Often unrealistic, but the model still works surprisingly well."},
{"id":"h45","level":2,"q":"Why does Naive Bayes work well for text classification?","a":"Text data is high-dimensional and sparse. Naive Bayes is computationally efficient, works well with sparse data, needs relatively little training data and handles large vocabularies."},
{"id":"h46","level":2,"q":"What is Laplace smoothing?","a":"It prevents zero probabilities for unseen words by adding a small count: P(word|class) = (count + 1) / (total + V), where V is the vocabulary size."},
{"id":"h47","level":2,"q":"Difference between Gaussian, Multinomial and Bernoulli Naive Bayes?","a":"Gaussian NB: continuous features. Multinomial NB: counts/frequencies (common for TF-IDF/count-based text). Bernoulli NB: binary features."},
{"id":"h48","level":2,"q":"Why does KNN suffer from the curse of dimensionality?","a":"In high dimensions points become sparse and distances less discriminative, so nearest neighbors may not be meaningfully closer than other points."},
{"id":"h49","level":2,"q":"Why is feature scaling critical for KNN?","a":"KNN depends directly on distance. With Age (20-60) and Salary (20,000-2,00,000), salary dominates Euclidean distance. Scaling stops one feature dominating just because of its range."},
{"id":"h50","level":2,"q":"How do you select K in KNN?","a":"Use validation or cross-validation. Small K: low bias, high variance. Large K: higher bias, lower variance. Choose K by validation performance, not a fixed rule."},
{"id":"h51","level":2,"q":"Why is K-Means sensitive to initialization?","a":"It minimizes a non-convex objective, so different initial centroids can lead to different local minima. K-Means++ improves initialization."},
{"id":"h52","level":2,"q":"Why does K-Means prefer spherical clusters?","a":"Its objective minimizes squared Euclidean distance to centroids, favoring compact, roughly spherical clusters. It struggles with elongated, irregular or varying-density clusters."},
{"id":"h53","level":2,"q":"How can you select K in K-Means?","a":"Elbow method, silhouette score, gap statistic, and domain knowledge."},
{"id":"h54","level":2,"q":"What does silhouette score measure?","a":"How well a point fits its own cluster compared with neighboring clusters: s(i) = (b(i) - a(i)) / max(a(i), b(i)), where a(i) is average distance within its cluster and b(i) the lowest average distance to another cluster. Higher is better."},
{"id":"h55","level":2,"q":"When would you use DBSCAN instead of K-Means?","a":"When clusters have irregular shapes, noise/outliers exist, or the number of clusters is unknown. It finds dense regions using epsilon and minimum samples."},
{"id":"h56","level":2,"q":"Why does PCA use eigenvectors?","a":"PCA searches for directions of maximum variance. Eigenvectors of the covariance matrix give the principal directions and eigenvalues give the variance captured along them."},
{"id":"h57","level":2,"q":"Why should features usually be standardized before PCA?","a":"PCA is variance-based, so a feature with a large scale (e.g. Income 20,000-500,000 vs Age 18-60) would dominate unless scaled."},
{"id":"h58","level":2,"q":"Does PCA always improve model performance?","a":"No. PCA is unsupervised and maximizes variance, not predictive power, so it may remove information useful for prediction and sometimes decrease performance."},
{"id":"h59","level":2,"q":"PCA vs feature selection?","a":"Feature selection keeps a subset of original features. PCA creates new combinations (principal components), changing the representation and reducing interpretability."},
{"id":"h60","level":2,"q":"Why can PCA hurt interpretability?","a":"A principal component is a mix like PC1 = 0.4x1 + 0.3x2 - 0.7x3 + ..., which is not a directly understandable original feature."},
{"id":"h61","level":2,"q":"What is target encoding?","a":"Replacing a categorical value with statistics derived from the target (e.g. City = Delhi -> average target 0.72). Naive target encoding can cause severe leakage; use training data only, often with out-of-fold encoding."},
{"id":"h62","level":2,"q":"Why can target encoding cause leakage?","a":"If a validation/test example's own target contributes to its encoded feature, information about the answer enters the input. Learn encodings from training data only and apply them to validation/test."},
{"id":"h63","level":2,"q":"What is feature interaction?","a":"When the effect of one feature depends on another. Example: y = b0 + b1x1 + b2x2 + b3x1x2, where x1x2 is the interaction term."},
{"id":"h64","level":2,"q":"What is the difference between feature selection and feature extraction? (advanced)","a":"Feature selection keeps original features (e.g. SelectKBest). Feature extraction creates new representations (e.g. PCA)."},
{"id":"h65","level":2,"q":"What is mutual information?","a":"Measures how much knowing one variable reduces uncertainty about another. It can capture nonlinear relationships that correlation may miss."},
{"id":"h66","level":2,"q":"Why can accuracy be misleading for imbalanced datasets? (advanced)","a":"With 99,000 normal and 1,000 fraud, predicting all normal gives 99% accuracy with zero fraud detected. Use precision, recall, F1, PR-AUC, the confusion matrix and business cost."},
{"id":"h67","level":2,"q":"Why can SMOTE cause data leakage?","a":"If SMOTE is applied before the train-test split, synthetic samples may use information from samples that later appear in the test set. Correct: split first, apply SMOTE only on training data, train, then evaluate on the untouched test set."},
{"id":"h68","level":2,"q":"Why shouldn't you blindly use SMOTE?","a":"It interpolates minority examples and can create unrealistic points, amplify noise, create class overlap and perform poorly in high dimensions. Class weights or threshold tuning may sometimes be better."},
{"id":"h69","level":2,"q":"What is cost-sensitive learning?","a":"Assigning different costs to different mistakes instead of treating all errors equally. Example: False Negative = Rs 10,000 loss vs False Positive = Rs 100 loss, so the model should account for the higher cost of false negatives."},
{"id":"h70","level":2,"q":"ROC-AUC vs PR-AUC - when would you prefer PR-AUC?","a":"When the positive class is rare (fraud, disease, spam detection). ROC-AUC can look strong even when minority-class performance is poor."},
{"id":"h71","level":2,"q":"What is the difference between macro, micro and weighted F1?","a":"Macro F1: average F1 across classes equally. Micro F1: aggregate all predictions before computing the metric. Weighted F1: average F1 weighted by class support. For imbalanced multiclass problems report multiple averages."},
{"id":"h72","level":2,"q":"What is threshold tuning?","a":"The default threshold is often 0.5 but the best one depends on the business goal. Example: threshold 0.5 gives recall 70%, threshold 0.3 gives recall 90%; if false negatives are expensive, lower the threshold."},
{"id":"h73","level":2,"q":"Can AUC be high while accuracy is low?","a":"Yes. AUC evaluates ranking across thresholds, while accuracy depends on one chosen threshold. A model can rank well but use a poor threshold."},
{"id":"h74","level":2,"q":"What is calibration vs discrimination?","a":"Discrimination: can the model separate positives from negatives (ROC-AUC, PR-AUC). Calibration: do predicted probabilities match actual frequencies (Brier score, calibration curve). A model can discriminate well but be poorly calibrated."},
{"id":"h75","level":2,"q":"What is the Brier score?","a":"Brier = (1/N) * sum((p_i - y_i)^2). Measures accuracy of probabilistic predictions; lower is better."},
{"id":"h76","level":2,"q":"Why shouldn't you tune hyperparameters on the test set?","a":"The test set would influence model selection and no longer be an unbiased estimate of generalization. Correct: cross-validation on training data -> tune -> final model -> test set once."},
{"id":"h77","level":2,"q":"What is nested cross-validation? (advanced)","a":"Separates hyperparameter selection (inner CV) from final performance estimation (outer CV). Useful for small datasets with extensive model selection."},
{"id":"h78","level":2,"q":"When should you use GroupKFold?","a":"When multiple samples belong to the same entity, e.g. 10 records from Patient A. They must not be split between train and validation, or the model may memorize patient-specific patterns."},
{"id":"h79","level":2,"q":"Why is random K-Fold inappropriate for time-series data?","a":"Random splits let future observations influence training, making evaluation unrealistic. Use time-based splits, expanding-window or walk-forward validation."},
{"id":"h80","level":2,"q":"What is temporal leakage?","a":"Using information from the future to predict the past, e.g. predicting January sales using February sales."},
{"id":"h81","level":2,"q":"Why should scaling be handled carefully in time-series ML?","a":"Fitting the scaler on the full dataset lets future information influence past observations. Fit on the training period, then transform validation/test periods."},
{"id":"h82","level":2,"q":"What is walk-forward validation?","a":"Repeatedly train on past data and validate on the next period, e.g. train Jan-Mar/validate Apr, train Jan-Apr/validate May, train Jan-May/validate Jun. It mimics production."},
{"id":"h83","level":2,"q":"Your training accuracy is 99% but validation accuracy is 70%. What do you investigate?","a":"Overfitting, train-validation distribution mismatch, data leakage, duplicates, incorrect preprocessing, label noise, class imbalance, validation split strategy, hyperparameters and feature leakage - not just the model itself."},
{"id":"h84","level":2,"q":"Training and validation accuracy are both low. What does that indicate?","a":"Often underfitting. Causes: model too simple, insufficient features, excessive regularization, poor feature representation, optimization problems."},
{"id":"h85","level":2,"q":"Training accuracy is low but test accuracy is high. Is that possible?","a":"Yes, but investigate. Reasons: randomness in small datasets, train/test distribution differences, augmentation, regularization during training, evaluation pipeline differences. If the gap is large, verify the evaluation process."},
{"id":"h86","level":2,"q":"Your validation score is excellent but production performance is poor. Why?","a":"Distribution shift, data drift, concept drift, leakage, training-serving skew, production preprocessing differences, unrepresentative validation set, or feedback loops."},
{"id":"h87","level":2,"q":"What is training-serving skew?","a":"Features are processed differently in training and production. Example: training uses a properly fitted StandardScaler, but production sends raw values, so the model sees a different representation."},
{"id":"h88","level":2,"q":"What is data drift? (advanced)","a":"The distribution of input features changes: P_train(X) != P_production(X). Example: a shopping model trained on 2025 data sees very different customer behavior in 2026."},
{"id":"h89","level":2,"q":"What is concept drift? (advanced)","a":"The relationship between inputs and target, P(Y|X), changes over time. Example: a fraud pattern common last year becomes rare after attackers change behavior."},
{"id":"h90","level":2,"q":"Difference between covariate shift and concept drift?","a":"Covariate shift: P(X) changes but P(Y|X) stays roughly stable. Concept drift: P(Y|X) itself changes."},
{"id":"h91","level":2,"q":"What is convex optimization?","a":"An optimization problem where every local minimum is also a global minimum. Convex objectives are generally easier to optimize reliably (e.g. ordinary linear regression, many regularized convex models)."},
{"id":"h92","level":2,"q":"Why is convexity useful in machine learning?","a":"With a convex objective there are no bad local minima, giving stronger guarantees of reaching the global optimum."},
{"id":"h93","level":2,"q":"What is the Hessian matrix?","a":"The matrix of second-order partial derivatives, H_ij = d^2 f / (dx_i dx_j). It describes curvature and is used by Newton's method."},
{"id":"h94","level":2,"q":"Gradient Descent vs Newton's Method?","a":"Gradient Descent: theta_new = theta - eta * grad J (first-order). Newton's method: theta_new = theta - H^(-1) * grad J (second-order). Newton can converge faster but computing/inverting the Hessian is expensive."},
{"id":"h95","level":2,"q":"What is a learning rate schedule?","a":"Changing the learning rate during training instead of keeping it constant, e.g. 0.1 -> 0.01 -> 0.001. A decreasing rate helps optimization converge more precisely."},
{"id":"h96","level":2,"q":"Why does TF-IDF outperform raw word counts in many classical NLP problems?","a":"TF-IDF reduces the importance of words that appear in many documents and emphasizes words specific to a document (TFIDF = TF x IDF), giving better representations for classical text classification."},
{"id":"h97","level":2,"q":"Why does Naive Bayes work well with TF-IDF or bag-of-words?","a":"It handles high-dimensional sparse features efficiently and estimates class-specific word distributions, making it effective for many traditional text-classification tasks."},
{"id":"h98","level":2,"q":"Why is cosine similarity commonly used for text?","a":"Text vectors are sparse and high-dimensional. Cosine similarity = (x . y) / (||x|| ||y||) measures the angle between vectors, focusing on direction rather than magnitude."},
{"id":"h99","level":2,"q":"What is the difference between word embeddings and TF-IDF?","a":"TF-IDF: sparse, based on word importance, no semantic understanding, interpretable. Embeddings: dense, capture semantic relationships, represent words/documents in continuous vector spaces."},
{"id":"h100","level":2,"q":"Why can TF-IDF become extremely high-dimensional?","a":"With a vocabulary of 100,000 unique words, each document can have 100,000 dimensions. Most values are zero, so the matrix is sparse."},
{"id":"h101","level":2,"q":"How would you design an ML pipeline from raw data to production?","a":"Raw Data -> Data Validation -> Cleaning -> Feature Engineering -> Train/Validation Split -> Training -> Hyperparameter Tuning -> Evaluation -> Model Serialization -> Deployment -> Monitoring -> Retraining. Key: make preprocessing reproducible between training and inference."},
{"id":"h102","level":2,"q":"What would you monitor after deploying an ML model?","a":"Prediction latency, error rate, input distribution, missing values, feature drift, prediction distribution, model performance when labels arrive, resource usage, and business KPIs."},
{"id":"h103","level":2,"q":"How would you detect model degradation if labels arrive months later?","a":"Monitor proxy signals: input drift, prediction distribution, confidence distribution, missing-value rates and feature statistics. When labels arrive, compute real performance metrics."},
{"id":"h104","level":2,"q":"How would you safely retrain a production model?","a":"New data -> validate -> train candidate -> compare with current model -> offline evaluation -> shadow/canary deployment -> monitor -> promote if acceptable. This reduces the risk of replacing a working model with a worse one."},
{"id":"h105","level":2,"q":"What is a shadow deployment?","a":"The new model receives production traffic and generates predictions, but its predictions don't affect users or decisions. Its performance can be compared safely with the existing model."},
{"id":"h106","level":2,"q":"What is a canary deployment?","a":"The new model is exposed to a small percentage of traffic first (e.g. current 95%, new 5%). If it behaves properly, traffic is gradually increased."},
{"id":"h107","level":2,"q":"What is model rollback?","a":"Reverting to the previous known-good model version if a new deployment causes problems. Production systems should therefore maintain model versions."},
{"id":"h108","level":2,"q":"Design a spam email detection system.","a":"Email -> text cleaning -> TF-IDF/embeddings -> classifier -> spam probability -> threshold -> Spam/Not Spam. Consider class imbalance, false positives, false negatives, new spam patterns, concept drift, latency and monitoring."},
{"id":"h109","level":2,"q":"How would you improve a spam classifier that has too many false positives?","a":"Inspect false-positive examples, analyze precision, tune the threshold, improve training data, add informative features, check label quality, compare alternative models and monitor production examples. Don't simply choose a more complex model."},
{"id":"h110","level":2,"q":"How would you design a fraud detection ML system?","a":"Transaction -> feature generation -> fraud model -> fraud probability -> risk threshold -> Approve/Review/Block. Issues: extreme class imbalance, delayed labels, concept drift, false-positive cost, real-time inference, temporal leakage, monitoring."},
{"id":"h111","level":2,"q":"Your model has 95% accuracy. Your manager says the model is good. Do you agree?","a":"Not immediately. Ask about class distribution, business objective, precision and recall, the baseline, cost of false positives/negatives and how the test set was built. Accuracy alone is insufficient."},
{"id":"h112","level":2,"q":"Your model's cross-validation score is 92%, but the final test score is 75%. What could be wrong?","a":"Test distribution differs, overfitting during model selection, leakage in CV, small test set, hyperparameter-tuning overfit, train/test preprocessing mismatch, or temporal/group leakage. Investigate the split and pipeline before changing the algorithm."},
{"id":"h113","level":2,"q":"You have 10 million rows but only 500 labeled examples. What would you do?","a":"Collect more labels, semi-supervised learning, active learning, transfer learning, weak supervision, careful cross-validation, simpler models and domain-specific feature engineering. Avoid blindly training a highly complex model on 500 labels."},
{"id":"h114","level":2,"q":"Your model performs well offline but users complain about predictions. What do you investigate?","a":"Whether test data represents real users, label quality, production preprocessing, data drift, threshold choice, segment-specific performance, latency/timeouts and feedback loops. Aggregate accuracy can hide poor performance for specific groups."},
{"id":"h115","level":2,"q":"Two models have identical accuracy. How would you choose between them?","a":"Compare precision/recall, calibration, latency, memory usage, interpretability, robustness, maintenance complexity, inference cost and stability under drift, based on actual application requirements."}
];
